The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own table shows more:
two causes, and a footnote about reports that moved by one day. This page splits the ticket by
what the reports say, so each problem has its own ticket and its own fix.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the <code>parser</code> problem. The other two stay open until
their own fixes ship.
</Callout>

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes
  the day first. 7 April goes out as 4 July.
- **`hint`**: the `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people type what it
  says, and the form reads the date day first. Only through that form, at three tenants. A
  parser fix does not touch it.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swap: Tuesday in Lisbon, Monday in Toronto.
  It points to a time-zone conversion. The desk noted these reports and filed them under the
  parser by tenant.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject line alone. All
three problems come in under the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so
the subject rule put them in one ticket. Intake's tag did not help: it is a guess from the
reporter's browser, and the desk's cause comes from the tenant.

What the desk saw, as the #4471 page shows it:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the desk

- Count reports by cause, not by subject (`sameCause`, try it on the [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe)),
  and read the body: a one-day move gets the parser's cause from its tenant, but it is not a swap.
- Decision 0001 is accepted, so it stays as written. Replacing it needs a new record.
- The export repeats some reports across its pages. Count unique ids, as the tables here do.

